\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Isotropy pieces of the background parameter set}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a fixed incidence presentation #{\Pi}, let #{K_\Pi} be its
finite group of signed endpoint automorphisms, acting on the
[parameter set](fdip-005E) #{\mathcal B_\Pi}. The isotropy group
at #{b} is #{(K_\Pi)_b=\{g:g b=b\}}. Partition
#{\mathcal B_\Pi} by the conjugacy class in #{K_\Pi} of this
stabilizer. These are its \newvocab{isotropy pieces}. If #{b'=g b},
then #{(K_\Pi)_{b'}=g(K_\Pi)_b g^{-1}}, so an orbit stays
inside one piece. Between different presentations, graph arrows
still identify the automorphism groups, without placing all of
them inside one common acting group.}

\p{Write #{b_{a,b}} for the alternating four-cycle with positive
weights #{(a,b,a,b)}, fixed #{m>0}, and retained pair #{\{0,2\}}.
The [signed stabilizer calculation](fdip-005I) gives
##{\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathsf{Bg}^{\pm}}(b_{a,b})
 \cong\begin{cases}
 C_2&a\ne b,\\
 C_2\times C_2&a=b.
 \end{cases}}
This is a change of isotropy at equal weights. In the directed
groupoid the uniformly oriented unit cycle admits only rotations;
preserving #{\{0,2\}} leaves the identity and half-turn, so its
stabilizer is #{C_2}. Reflections belong to the larger signed
convention.}

\p{The orthogonal stabilizers of the same pair #{(Q,P_R)} are
#{(C_2)^2} and #{(C_2)^3}, as proved in \ref{fdip-005I}.
They describe a different comparison and do not define the graph
isotropy pieces above. Here \em{strata} can mean only this
set-level partition by isotropy type. No smooth stratification,
analytic singularity or physical phase transition follows from
it. Those further interpretations require additional structure
and arguments.}
